+---
+node: record-store
+---
+
+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edits Use Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+## Context
+
+Record-store defines how independently edited copies of the same plot visit become one current record. Workers on shared tablets correct visits days after fetching their starting state, sometimes after comparing field notes with a colleague who edited the same visit from a different tablet. The server cannot treat arrival order as authority, and two credible policies exist. This decision sizes the submission envelope for tablet-sync, the review workflow and export eligibility rules for review-export, and the record states this node exposes.
+
+The stake that makes this live rather than obvious: the two policies trade review workload against the integrity of a worker's submitted visit, and the failed policy is invisible until review volume or data quality degrades in ways the coordinators attribute to something else.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+Gates:
+
+- **No silently discarded edits** (inherited; HLD tenet *Do not silently discard a submitted edit*). Any policy that overwrites or loses a submitted payload fails.
+- **No server inference of scientific relationships** (doc-local). The team rejected merge logic that combines field edits without being able to show what it combined; a policy whose core mechanism is automated cross-submission field combination fails.
+
+Weighted criteria:
+
+- **Preserve whole-visit scientific intent** — *major* (tenet: *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*). An edit to a species identification and its cover estimate is one observation, not two independent facts; a mechanism that may split it must be weighed against this.
+- **Coordinator auditability of every stored value** — *major* (tenet: *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*). Coordinators must be able to say for each exported value where it came from without reverse-engineering merge rules.
+- **Review queue size** — *moderate*. Six thousand visits per season with two coordinators; every visit touched by competing edits lands in the queue under whole-visit revisions, only genuinely overlapping ones under field-aware merge.
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden** — *moderate* (tenet: *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator* — extended here to system behavior the team itself must maintain). Merge rules are standing code the team owns and must update as the survey's fields evolve.
+- **Submission payload size and tablet storage** — *minor*. Whole-visit payloads are larger than deltas, but a visit is a small structured record; the tablets already hold the full visit for display.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description**: Each accepted submission stores a complete visit payload. The submitter names the revision it started from (`base_revision_id`). A submission based on the current tip advances the visit silently; anything else becomes a competing complete revision and marks the visit unresolved until a coordinator adopts one. No submission is ever combined, overwritten, or discarded; resolution and later edits only change which revision is current.
+
+**Demonstration**: Worker A and Worker B both base edits on revision 3 — A corrects an observer name, B adds a missing species. Both arrive as complete revisions 4 and 5; the visit is unresolved. A coordinator opens the comparison view, sees both visits whole, adopts revision 5, and the resolution records both submission IDs.
+
+**Analysis**:
+- No silently discarded edits: **passes** — every submission is retained permanently; nothing is combined away.
+- No server inference of scientific relationships: **passes** — the server never combines anything; scientific relationships stay inside one worker's submitted visit.
+- Whole-visit scientific intent: **strong** — a revision is exactly what the worker saw and submitted.
+- Coordinator auditability: **strong** — an exported value is a named revision with a named author.
+- Review queue size: **weak** — unrelated-field edits still produce competing revisions and review work; the team accepts a larger queue as the price.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **strong** — no merge rules to write or maintain; the logic is one comparison of base revision IDs.
+- Payload size and tablet storage: **partial** — full payloads on every edit, tolerable at this record size.
+
+**Summary**: Buys auditability and simple mechanics by paying in review volume.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description**: Each edit supplies its base state and structured per-field changes. The server automatically combines changes to disjoint fields into a merged current record; only fields changed by more than one worker go to review. Review-export must present field-level provenance — which submission each exported value came from — and the merge rules (for scalar fields, observer lists, species entries) are maintained, versioned logic.
+
+**Demonstration**: Worker A corrects an observer name, Worker B adds a missing species, both from revision 3's state. The merge rules see disjoint fields and combine them: the current record has A's observer and B's species without review. If both revised the same species' cover estimate, that field goes to review with field-level provenance.
+
+**Analysis**:
+- No silently discarded edits: **passes** — with the caveat that combined fields may not be distinguishable from deliberately coordinated edits.
+- No server inference of scientific relationships: **eliminated** — the mechanism's core is exactly such inference; the user declined rules that combine edits "without noticing," and species-level fields are where the inference is most likely to err.
+- Whole-visit scientific intent: **weak** — disjoint-field combination can split related fields (identification and cover estimate) across submissions into a record no worker authored.
+- Coordinator auditability: **partial** — field-level provenance can show sources, but a merged record itself is a server artifact no worker stands behind.
+- Review queue size: **strong** — only overlapping fields create review work.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **weak** — maintained rules for every field type, updated as the survey evolves.
+- Payload size and tablet storage: **strong** — structured changes are smaller; tablets keep base snapshots either way.
+
+**Summary**: Buys review volume by paying in maintained merge logic and the risk of scientifically incoherent automatic combinations.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Chosen: whole-visit revisions.** The elimination gate is decisive on its own — the team explicitly prefers a bigger review queue over maintained merge rules that can combine scientifically related edits without the server noticing — and whole-visit revisions also dominate on both major criteria: what is exported is a revision a named worker submitted whole, coordinators can trace every value without understanding any rules.
+
+The tradeoff accepted: review volume grows with every concurrent edit, including trivially unrelated ones, and review-export's export set excludes unresolved visits until a coordinator acts. The alternative remains credible if review volume at that scale proves unmanageable for two coordinators; re-opening it then would mean re-deriving this node's submission envelope and review-export's provenance design, not a drop-in swap.
+
+Committing obligates downstream nodes:
+
+- **tablet-sync (cascade)**: submissions carry a base revision ID, not a delta or base snapshot; the tablet tracks each visit's last-known revision ID and must explain unresolved-visit outcomes (competing revisions pending) to field workers, including that their edit is retained and pending rather than lost.
+- **review-export (cascade)**: the unresolved-work representation is the competing-revision list; the review side needs a revision comparison view across whole visits, resolution adopts one revision as submitted, and unresolved visits are held out of the CSV export set.
+
+The decision turns on the HLD tenets *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* and *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*, taken jointly against the field-aware option's auditability and intent-preservation weaknesses.
+
+## References
+
+- docs/intent/record-store/record-store-design.md — the revision model this decision underwrites
+- docs/high-level-design.md — HLD tenets cited in Selection
